  "details": "This is the feedback on synchronisation for strokes from the item “Frosted Scribe”.\n\nSynchronising pen strokes using only a boolean indicating whether the user is drawing, along with the pen’s position and rotation data, is likely to result in inaccurate stroke reproduction on other clients. This approach is highly sensitive to differences in client frame rates, network synchronisation sampling, interpolation performed by the synchronisation system, and packet loss, which can prevent the intended line from being reproduced correctly for other users.\n\nIn practice, I have personally encountered situations where this approach resulted in strokes not being accurately synchronised across clients, making it difficult for other users to understand what was being drawn.\n\nFurthermore, when drawing glyphs that require a high density of strokes and precise control, such as kanji characters, the resulting shapes were sometimes distorted to the point of being unreadable.\n\nAs a potential improvement, it may be better to collect and finalise the vertex data that represents a stroke at the moment the user finishes drawing, and then transmit this data to other users over the network. Each client could then redraw the stroke based on the received vertex data, which should help achieve more consistent and accurate line reproduction across clients.",
